---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('privy')!;
const b = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/privy-vs-postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/privy-vs-postscript');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should Privy replace Postscript?",
    "answer": "Decide whether the job is onsite capture, SMS sending, or both. Privy documents a Postscript subscriber-sync integration, so keeping Privy for capture and Postscript for SMS is a configuration to evaluate rather than assuming one must replace the other."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does subscriber sync coordinate cart messages?",
    "answer": "A subscriber arriving in Postscript does not demonstrate shared cart queues, purchase exits, discount eligibility or frequency limits. Test those separately before enabling overlapping flows."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can an email signup receive recovery SMS?",
    "answer": "An email signup alone does not establish SMS marketing permission. Review the actual phone collection disclosure and resulting subscription state; do not treat a populated phone field as permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should a signup offer reach SMS?",
    "answer": "Record the exact promise made on the form, its eligibility rules and expiry. Check the destination welcome message against that promise, and verify the code at checkout before using it in a cart reminder."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Compare capture and messaging together for the same audience and intended activity. Include any retained email tool, SMS usage and applicable fees, integration maintenance and migration work. Use current account quotes rather than an assumed entry price."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should be measured?",
    "answer": "Separate form completion, eligible subscriber arrival, welcome delivery and cart purchases. Deduplicate orders across tools and compare net economics; attributed revenue alone does not establish incremental recovery."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Privy vs Postscript for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Choose who captures the subscriber, who owns SMS, and how a signup promise survives the handoff to cart recovery.</p>
      <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Capture decision', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate the onsite signup experience and destination sync.' }, { label: 'SMS decision', tool: b.name, reason: 'Evaluate the receiving SMS workflow and its relationship to existing email.' }]} />
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the existing stack</h2>
        <p>A store already using Postscript may need a better signup experience rather than a replacement sender. A store using Privy for capture may instead be deciding where new phone subscribers should go. These are different purchases: improving a form does not by itself improve cart recovery, and moving a sender does not repair a confusing signup offer.</p>
        <p>Privy describes its <a href="https://www.privy.com/integrations/postscript">Postscript integration</a> as a way to collect phone numbers through Privy displays and sync subscribers to Postscript. Its setup outline includes connecting the account, updating a display, adding a sync rule and confirming the destination. This supports evaluating a split capture/sending setup. It does not establish that the two tools coordinate recovery messages.</p>
        <h2>Compare three configurations</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Decision to resolve</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Retain the current Privy setup</td><td>Your capture and messaging requirements are already met</td><td>Identify the missing SMS requirement before adding another subscription or sender.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Privy capture with Postscript SMS</td><td>You want to retain the display while evaluating Postscript as the SMS destination</td><td>Validate sync rules, subscription state, offer continuity and ownership of each message.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Replace the capture setup as well</td><td>You want to remove a handoff or consolidate operational work</td><td>Compare the replacement form against actual mobile and desktop signup requirements before retiring the existing display.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Follow the form promise into the first message</h2>
        <p>Write down what a visitor sees: the incentive, eligible products, first-order restriction, expiry and whether email and SMS are separate choices. Then inspect the resulting destination record and welcome flow. A form promising a welcome code and a text promising a different offer can create a support problem even when subscriber sync succeeds.</p>
        <p>For an existing email subscriber adding a phone number, decide whether this is a new incentive entitlement or only a new channel subscription. Use the original signup promise and checkout eligibility rules to answer that question. A fresh SMS record should not silently override an already-used welcome offer.</p>
        <h2>Give every recovery message one owner</h2>
        <p>List existing email reminders, SMS reminders and welcome messages before enabling the connection. Assign the sender, trigger, eligibility check and purchase exit for each. Keep the email tool visible in this map even if this purchase only concerns SMS: a welcome text can overlap an email cart reminder without either tool knowing the other is queued.</p>
        <p>At message execution, check the latest subscription and purchase state available to that sender. Establish what happens when an update is delayed. If shared limits or cancellation cannot be demonstrated, simplify the active flow instead of describing the tools as automatically coordinated. Do not set a universal delay or touch count without considering the signup promise, order cycle and actual queue behavior.</p>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance cases for this handoff</h2>
        <p>These are checks to run in your own configuration, not observed product results. Use designated test records and inspect the form, destination record, event log and queue together.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Email only:</strong> submit the form without SMS enrollment. Confirm that the resulting record is not treated as permission for marketing texts.</li>
          <li><strong>New phone signup:</strong> verify the intended destination and subscription state, then check whether the welcome flow honors the displayed offer.</li>
          <li><strong>Existing subscriber:</strong> submit again and inspect whether the update creates a second welcome entry or changes incentive eligibility.</li>
          <li><strong>Purchase during handoff:</strong> complete a test order after signup but before a cart reminder becomes eligible. Inspect each sender's actual exit behavior.</li>
          <li><strong>Opt-out with queued work:</strong> check how the SMS sender handles pending messages and how the other tool receives or retains that state.</li>
          <li><strong>Disconnected sync:</strong> inspect the failure and recovery path, including whether a replay can duplicate an entry. Keep overlapping recovery flows paused until ownership is clear.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Protect the economics of the promised offer</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $411 basket with a 44% product gross margin has $230.16 product cost and $180.84 gross profit at full price. A 10% discount reduces revenue to $369.90. With the same product cost, gross profit becomes $139.74, a $41.10 reduction. This is before shipping subsidies, payment fees, messaging costs, returns and other expenses; it is not a forecast of either product's performance.</p>
        <p>Use that calculation when deciding whether the SMS welcome offer should also appear in cart recovery. Check stacking and first-order rules at checkout. Do not multiply gross profit by the discount percentage: the discount reduces selling revenue while the product cost remains.</p>
        <h2>Measure the handoff before declaring a recovery win</h2>
        <p>Track form completion separately from eligible destination arrivals. Next distinguish welcome delivery from later cart reminders, and deduplicate purchased orders across email and SMS reports. Otherwise a better form can appear to improve recovery simply because it supplies more identifiable visitors, while both tools claim the same purchase.</p>
        <p>If you need a causal comparison, propose an experiment around the actual change: capture design, SMS destination or recovery sequence. Keep the other elements stable where practical and choose a comparison period and sample appropriate to your traffic. Do not promise a recovery lift or a universal holdout percentage.</p>
        <h2>Cost and rollout decision</h2>
        <p>Compare the full retained stack: capture subscription, email subscription, SMS charges and applicable fees, plus time spent maintaining the sync. Ask for a current quote at the same audience size and expected message activity. A low capture price does not describe the cost of a split sending setup.</p>
        <p>Before changing the live form, record its current destinations and preserve a rollback configuration. Validate the new handoff before retiring the old route, then remove duplicate ownership deliberately. Check how collected subscribers and pending entries will be handled if you revert. This is a proposed rollout sequence, not a claim that either product migrates pending work automatically.</p>
        <h2>When to evaluate another option</h2>
        <p>If the requirement is a different email automation workflow, include <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com">Sequenzy</a> in that separate evaluation. Confirm the actual Shopify events, consent handling and required integrations before adopting it. Adding a third sender does not resolve an unclear Privy-to-Postscript handoff.</p>
        <p>Continue with <a href="/compare/privy-vs-mailchimp">Privy vs Mailchimp</a> for an existing email audience decision or <a href="/compare/privy-vs-attentive">Privy vs Attentive</a> for another capture-to-SMS comparison.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Postscript FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
